<h1>HD2 Capabilities</h1>

<p>HD2 can author <strong>everything HD can</strong> — the editing surface is the same. What differs is how each element is written to disk. This page is the authoritative map of that decision: for every supported element, is it stored as <strong>Markdown</strong> or as a verbatim <strong>HTML island</strong>?</p>

<h2>Legend</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Markdown</strong> — stored using native Markdown syntax; reads and diffs like Markdown.</li>
  <li><strong>HTML island</strong> — stored as verbatim HTML inside the Markdown file, because Markdown can't express it losslessly.</li>
  <li><strong>Conditional</strong> — stored as Markdown in the simple case, and as an HTML island when it exceeds what Markdown can represent (the boundary is stated in the Notes).</li>
</ul>

<h2>Element map</h2>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6" cellspacing="0">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Element</th>
      <th>Stored as</th>
      <th>Notes</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td colspan="3"><strong>Text and inline</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>h1</code>–<code>h6</code></td><td>Markdown</td><td>ATX headings (<code>#</code>…<code>######</code>).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>p</code></td><td>Markdown</td><td>Plain paragraph.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>strong</code>, <code>b</code></td><td>Markdown</td><td><code>**…**</code>. The strong/b distinction is not preserved.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>em</code>, <code>i</code></td><td>Markdown</td><td><code>*…*</code>. The em/i distinction is not preserved.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>s</code>, <code>del</code></td><td>Markdown</td><td><code>~~…~~</code> (GFM). Lossless in HD2 — an upgrade over HD, where strikethrough is raw HTML.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>code</code> (inline)</td><td>Markdown</td><td>Backticks.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>a</code></td><td>Markdown</td><td><code>[text](href)</code>. <code>target</code>, <code>rel</code>, and <code>download</code> are dropped.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>u</code></td><td>HTML island</td><td>Markdown has no underline syntax.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>mark</code></td><td>HTML island</td><td>No Markdown highlight syntax.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>sub</code>, <code>sup</code></td><td>HTML island</td><td>No standard Markdown.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>kbd</code>, <code>samp</code>, <code>var</code>, <code>abbr</code>, <code>cite</code>, <code>q</code>, <code>small</code></td><td>HTML island</td><td>No Markdown equivalents.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>span</code></td><td>HTML island</td><td>Carries attributes/styling Markdown can't hold.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>br</code></td><td>Markdown</td><td>Hard line break.</td></tr>

    <tr><td colspan="3"><strong>Blocks</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>blockquote</code></td><td>Markdown</td><td><code>&gt;</code>-prefixed lines.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>pre</code> + <code>code</code></td><td>Markdown</td><td>Fenced code block. <code>class="language-…"</code> becomes the info string.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>ul</code>, <code>ol</code>, <code>li</code></td><td>Markdown</td><td>Bulleted / numbered lists. <code>ol start</code> is preserved (GFM).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>hr</code></td><td>Markdown</td><td><code>---</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>dl</code>, <code>dt</code>, <code>dd</code></td><td>HTML island</td><td>No standard Markdown for definition lists.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>details</code>, <code>summary</code></td><td>HTML island</td><td>No standard Markdown for disclosure widgets.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>div</code></td><td>HTML island</td><td>Generic container; carries attributes/styling.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>section</code>, <code>article</code>, <code>aside</code>, <code>header</code>, <code>footer</code>, <code>nav</code>, <code>main</code></td><td>HTML island</td><td>Sectioning wrappers. The <strong>entire subtree</strong> is stored as HTML (see below).</td></tr>

    <tr><td colspan="3"><strong>Media</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>img</code></td><td>Conditional</td><td>Markdown <code>![alt](src)</code> when bare. Becomes an HTML island when it carries <code>width</code>, <code>height</code>, <code>style</code>, or <code>class</code>, so styling survives.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>figure</code>, <code>figcaption</code></td><td>HTML island</td><td>Markdown has no figure/caption construct.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>svg</code></td><td>HTML island</td><td>Inline SVG passes through unchanged.</td></tr>

    <tr><td colspan="3"><strong>Tables</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>table</code> (simple)</td><td>Markdown</td><td>GFM table when rectangular, with a header row, single-span cells, and inline-only cell content.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>table</code> (complex)</td><td>HTML island</td><td>Any <code>colspan</code>/<code>rowspan</code>, block/multi-paragraph cell content, non-rectangular shape, or missing header row keeps the whole table as HTML.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>thead</code>, <code>tbody</code>, <code>tfoot</code>, <code>tr</code>, <code>th</code>, <code>td</code>, <code>caption</code>, <code>colgroup</code>, <code>col</code></td><td>Conditional</td><td>Follow the parent table's ruling — Markdown when the table is GFM-expressible, HTML otherwise.</td></tr>

    <tr><td colspan="3"><strong>Interactive controls (HD2-only)</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Checkbox / task list</td><td>Markdown</td><td>GFM <code>- [x]</code> / <code>- [ ]</code>. Stored as <code>&lt;li data-type="task" data-checked&gt;</code>; interactive in the editor. See <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="interactive-controls.hd">Interactive Controls</a>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Radio group (unnamed)</td><td>Markdown</td><td>HD2 syntax <code>- (x)</code> / <code>- ( )</code>. One list = one single-select, deselectable group.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Radio group (named)</td><td>HTML island</td><td>A <code>data-group</code> name can't live in the paren markers, so a named group is kept as an HTML block.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Cell alignment in Markdown tables</h2>

<p>When a simple table is stored as GFM, per-column alignment is preserved through the delimiter row. A header cell whose <code>align</code> attribute or <code>text-align</code> style is <code>left</code>, <code>center</code>, or <code>right</code> produces <code>:---</code>, <code>:---:</code>, or <code>---:</code> respectively. Other cell attributes (<code>scope</code>, <code>headers</code>, <code>class</code>) are not expressible in GFM and are dropped; if you need them preserved, the table must be a complex (HTML-island) table.</p>

<h2>Two behaviors to keep in mind</h2>

<h3>Block wrappers are self-contained HTML</h3>

<p>When a sectioning element such as <code>&lt;section&gt;</code> becomes an HTML island, its entire contents are stored as HTML rather than as Markdown nested inside the wrapper. Keep prose you want stored as Markdown outside of these wrappers.</p>

<h3>Attributes on Markdown-native elements</h3>

<p>Markdown syntax has nowhere to hold <code>id</code>, <code>class</code>, <code>style</code>, or <code>data-*</code>. When one of those attributes matters on an otherwise Markdown-native element, promote the element to a form that stays an HTML island — for example a styled <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> (which HD2 already keeps as HTML) or wrapping the content in a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>/<code>&lt;span&gt;</code>. On a plain heading, paragraph, or list, such attributes are not currently retained through a round-trip.</p>

<h2>Relationship to the HD spec</h2>

<p>HD2's boundary is the natural extension of HD's Markdown round-trip table in the <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="../format-spec.hd">HD Format Specification</a>. In HD that table describes a one-way "Copy as Markdown"; in HD2 the same classification governs how the file is stored on every save. Elements HD marks as <em>Lossless</em> are stored as Markdown; those it marks <em>Raw HTML</em> become islands; the <em>Lossy</em> cases (tables, images) become the <strong>Conditional</strong> rules above.</p>
